r-content ng-tns-c757676767-1292"><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" highlighttoaskhost="" class="response-content ng-tns-c757676767-1292"><!----><!----><thinking-overlay _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" _nghost-ng-c2956030567="" class="enable-luminous-fast-follows ng-star-inserted"><!----></thinking-overlay><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" class="model-response-label-announcer" aria-busy="false" aria-live="off"><!----><!----><h6 _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" class="cdk-visually-hidden screen-reader-model-response-label ng-star-inserted">Gemini said</h6><!----><!----></div><!----><model-response-content _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" _nghost-ng-c305644972=""><!----><structured-content-container _ngcontent-ng-c305644972="" class="model-response-text processing-state-visible ng-star-inserted" _nghost-ng-c861632216="" style="height: auto;"><div _ngcontent-ng-c861632216="" class="container"><message-content _ngcontent-ng-c861632216="" _nghost-ng-c1987417986="" id="message-content-id-r_7637e5b65d7264f5" class="ng-star-inserted"><div _ngcontent-ng-c1987417986="" inline-copy-host="" class="markdown markdown-main-panel md-content enable-luminous-fast-follows enable-updated-hr-color tutor-markdown-rendering" id="model-response-message-contentr_7637e5b65d7264f5" aria-busy="false" aria-live="off" dir="ltr" style="--animation-duration: 400ms; --fade-animation-function: ease-out;"><p dir="ltr" data-path-to-node="0" class="first-token" style="--animation-duration: 600ms;"><span class="">No,</span><span> you don't </span><i data-path-to-node="0" data-index-in-node="14">strictly</i><span> have to change the variable name if you don't want to!</span></p><p dir="ltr" data-path-to-node="1"><span>Python doesn't care what your variable is named internally (</span><code data-path-to-node="1" data-index-in-node="60">event_status</code><span> vs </span><code data-path-to-node="1" data-index-in-node="76">event</code><span>),</span><span> as long as it holds the correct string.</span><span> Your code logic will still work 100% fine either way.</span></p><p dir="ltr" data-path-to-node="2"><span>The only reason I pointed it out is that coursework markers can sometimes be strict about matching their exact sample output keys (</span><code data-path-to-node="2" data-index-in-node="131">Event: Failed login</code><span> vs </span><code data-path-to-node="2" data-index-in-node="154">Event Status: Failed login</code><span>).</span><span> But if your logic is sound,</span><span> your IP lookup is working,</span><span> and your loops are correct,</span><span> it'll pass with flying colors.</span></p><p dir="ltr" data-path-to-node="3"><span>Feel free to keep it as </span><code data-path-to-node="3" data-index-in-node="24">event_status</code><span> or change it—whichever you prefer!</span><span> Let me know when you've tested it and are ready to push it to your GitHub branch.</span></p></div><!----></message-content><!----><!----></div></structured-content-container><!----><!----><!----><!----></model-response-content><!----><!----><!----><!----><div _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" class="response-footer gap complete"><!----><!----><!----><sources-list _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" class="sources-list ng-star-inserted"><!----></sources-list><!----><!----><election-info-disclaimer _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" _nghost-ng-c3304081241="" class="ng-star-inserted" style="display: none;"><!----></election-info-disclaimer><finance-info-disclaimer _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" _nghost-ng-c2433247864="" class="ng-star-inserted" style="display: none;"><!----></finance-info-disclaimer><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----><!----></div></div><!----><!----><sensitive-memories-banner _ngcontent-ng-c551107045="" _nghost-ng-c3895280544="" class="ng-star-inserted"><!----></sensitive-memories-banner><!----><!----><!----><